{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, shorten causal readout pooling from the most recent four states to the most recent two, retaining the 0.8 current-state + 0.2 pooled-state blend. Short histories may benefit from less smoothing across older interests. Pool only observed prefix states during training and inference; retain final-real-position readout. Keep features, embeddings, causal blocks, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed. Update readout metadata and predictor-writer checks; retain one model per category.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, replace the six-block dilation schedule [1,2,4,8,1,2] with [1,2,4,1,2,4]. Repeated local-to-medium causal processing may better capture dependencies in histories with median length 7–8 while reducing sparse long-range convolution. Retain six blocks, kernel size, residual scale 0.5, features, embeddings, history/readout, scoring, loss and optimizer. Synchronize architecture metadata and predictor construction; train one model per category.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, jointly lower learning rate from 6e-4 to 4e-4 and increase dropout from 0.10 to 0.15. Smaller updates coupled with stronger regularization may improve generalization without increasing model capacity, following the regression at width 64. Retain width 48, six causal blocks, existing features, tying, scoring, readout, loss and Adam optimizer. Keep seed 42, epoch cap 200 and 200 batches per epoch; save one checkpoint per category.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, reduce tied item embedding width and matching hidden width from 48 to 40, propagating only required tensor-shape changes. Since width 64 regressed, modestly reduced capacity may regularize sparse item representations while preserving beneficial input/output sharing. Keep initialization scheme, features, causal block structure, history/readout, dot-product scoring, loss and optimizer fixed. Save width metadata and ensure the predictor writer reconstructs the matching single model per category."
}
